#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
Section 2 English translations (Paras 26-50) for Gabriel Marcel's
"Positions et approches concrètes du mystère ontologique"
"""

def get_sec2():
    return [
        # Para 26
        "These preliminary reflections on the ontological exigence, by bringing out its indeterminacy, reveal an essential paradox; to formulate this exigence is first of all to enter into a labyrinth of problems: Does being exist? What is being? But upon these problems I cannot bring my reflection to bear without seeing a new abyss open beneath my feet: I who question about being, can I be assured that I am?",
        
        # Para 27
        "And yet, I who formulate this problem ought to be able to maintain myself outside—whether on this side or beyond—this problem which I formulate. In reality it is clear that this is not so; reflection shows me that this problem inevitably invades, so to speak, this theoretically preserved proscenium. It is by a fiction that idealism under its traditional form attempts to maintain, on the margin of being, a consciousness that posits or denies it.",
        
        # Para 28
        "I cannot therefore exempt myself from asking at the same time: who am I, I who question about being? What qualification do I have to proceed with these investigations? If I am not, how can I hope to see them succeed? Even admitting that I am, how can I be assured that I am?",
        
        # Para 29
        "Contrary to the idea that might naturally present itself here, I think that on this level the cogito can be of no help to us. Whatever Descartes may have thought about it, if it puts us in possession of an indubitable, this indubitable concerns only the epistemological subject as the organ of objective knowledge. The cogito, as I have written elsewhere, guards the threshold of validity and that is all; the proof of this lies in the indeterminacy bearing upon the 'I.' The 'I am' presents itself, it seems to me, as an indecomposable whole.",
        
        # Para 30
        "An objection remains to be foreseen: one of two things, it will be said: either the being aimed at by the question 'What am I?' concerns the subject of knowledge, but in that case we are precisely on the level of the cogito;",
        
        # Para 31
        "or else what you call the ontological exigence is only the extreme point—if not the fallacious transposition—of a properly vital exigence with which the metaphysician does not have to concern himself.",
        
        # Para 32
        "But does not the error consist here in the first place in arbitrarily severing the question 'Am I?' from the ontological 'problem' taken in its entirety? In reality, the two can only be grasped and approached simultaneously. But equally, as we shall see, they destroy themselves in some measure as problems.",
        
        # Para 33
        "It must be added that the Cartesian position, in fact, is inseparable from a dualism that for my part I should reject without hesitation. To posit the ontological problem is to interrogate oneself on the totality of being and on myself as a totality.",
        
        # Para 34
        "Moreover, and in a correlative manner, it is permissible to ask whether this dissociation of the intellectual and the vital, with the arbitrary depreciation or arbitrary exaltation of the vital that results from it, ought not to be precisely refused. There is certainly no ground for denying that it is legitimate to make distinctions of order within the unity of a living being who thinks and strives to think himself; but the ontological problem arises only beyond these distinctions and for this being grasped in his most comprehensive unity.",
        
        # Para 35
        "If we seek to master the situation at which we have arrived at this point in our reflection, we shall observe that we are in the presence of a certain impulse toward an affirmation which, in the last analysis, appears incapable of being established; for I can judge myself qualified to formulate it only on the basis of this affirmation itself.",
        
        # Para 36
        "Let us observe that this situation cannot arise where I find myself effectively in the presence of a problem to be solved; in that case, indeed, I work upon data, but at the same time everything proceeds, everything authorizes me to proceed as though I did not have to concern myself with this working self, as though it did not enter into account. It is a presupposition, and it is nothing more.",
        
        # Para 37
        "Here, on the contrary, what I shall call the ontological status of questioning assumes decisive importance. On the other hand, as long as I confine myself to reflection itself, it seems that I engage in a regression without end. Only, by the very fact that I conceive the impossibility of assigning an end to this regression, I surpass it in some measure; I recognize that this regressive process remains interior to a certain affirmation which I am rather than which I utter: in uttering it, I break it, I parcel it out, I prepare to betray it.",
        
        # Para 38
        "One might say in an inevitably approximate language that my questioning about being presupposes an affirmation in which I would be in some way passive, and of which I would be the seat rather than the subject. But this is only a limit, and one that I cannot realize without contradiction. I orient myself therefore toward the positing or the recognition of a participation that possesses a reality of subject; this participation cannot, by definition, be an object of thought; it cannot function as a solution, but figures beyond the world of problems: it is meta-problematic.",
        
        # Para 39
        "Conversely, one can see that if a meta-problematic can be affirmed, it is as transcending the opposition between a subject who would affirm being and being insofar as affirmed by this subject—and as grounding in some way this opposition. To posit a meta-problematic is to think the primacy of being in relation to knowledge (not of being affirmed, but rather of being affirming itself); it is to recognize that knowledge is enveloped by being, that it is in some way interior to it, in a sense perhaps analogous to that which Paul Claudel sought to define in his Art Poétique. From this point of view, contrary to what the theory of knowledge vainly strives to establish, there is truly a mystery of knowing; knowledge is suspended from a mode of participation of which no epistemology whatever can hope to give an account, because epistemology itself presupposes it.",
        
        # Para 40
        "At the point where we now stand, the distinction between problem and mystery can be stated precisely: a mystery is a problem that encroaches upon its own data, that invades them and thereby surpasses itself as a simple problem. A series of examples will permit us to confer upon this definition a graspable content. It is obvious that there is a mystery of the union of soul and body; the indivisible unity that is always inadequately expressed by formulas such as: 'I have a body,' 'I use my body,' 'I feel my body,' etc., is exterior to all analysis and cannot in any way be reconstituted by synthetic means from elements that would be logically prior to it; it is not merely given, I would say that it is giving (donnante) in the sense of what I shall call the presence of myself to myself, a presence of which the act of self-consciousness is in the end only an inadequate symbolization.",
        
        # Para 41
        "We see immediately that there is no hope of drawing an absolute line of demarcation between the problem and the mystery. For a mystery placed before reflection tends inevitably to degrade into a problem. This is particularly manifest in the case of the problem of evil.",
        
        # Para 42
        "Almost inevitably, indeed, I am led to treat evil as a disorder that I contemplate and whose causes or reason for being—or secret finality—I seek to disentangle. How does 'this machine' function in so defective a manner? Or does this apparent defectiveness stem from a defectiveness, this time real, of my vision? In this second case, it is in me that the true disorder would reside, but this disorder would remain objective in relation to my thought which detects or observes it. But evil purely observed or contemplated ceases to be evil suffered; quite simply it ceases to be evil. In reality I grasp it as evil only insofar as it affects me, that is to say, insofar as I am involved in it, in the sense in which one is involved in an affair; it is this involvement that is fundamental here, and I can abstract from it only by an unjustifiable fiction; for at the same stroke I proceed as though I were God, and a God of contemplation.",
        
        # Para 43
        "We clearly see the breakdown here of the distinction between what is in me and what is merely before me; it falls under the blows of a reductive reflection: a reflection to the second power.",
        
        # Para 44
        "But it goes without saying that it is in love that we see most clearly the erasure of the boundary between the 'in me' and the 'before me';—perhaps it would even be possible to show that the sphere of the meta-problematic coincides with that of love, that a mystery such as that of soul and body is graspable only beginning from love, and in some way expresses it.",
        
        # Para 45
        "It is, moreover, inevitable that a reflection that has not reflected upon itself, in attacking love, should tend to dissolve this meta-problematic, to interpret it in terms of abstract elements such as the will to live, the will to power, the libido, etc.; on the other hand, the order of problems being that of the valid, it would be extremely difficult—or even impossible—to refute these interpretations without placing oneself on a different terrain, on which in truth they lose their meaning. At the same time, I am assured, it is guaranteed to me—and this assurance envelops me like a protective mantle—that so far as I truly love, I do not have to worry about these depreciatory reductions.",
        
        # Para 46
        "It will be asked: what then is the criterion of real love? We must reply that there is a criteriology only in the order of the object or of the problematizable, but already we perceive at a distance the eminent value which, from the ontological point of view, ought to be assigned to fidelity.",
        
        # Para 47
        "Here is another example, more immediate, more particular, and which seems to me well fitted to shed light on the difference between a mystery and a problem.",
        
        # Para 48
        "You make an encounter that turns out to have a profound, indefinite repercussion upon your life. Everyone may have experienced what an encounter sometimes means from the spiritual point of view—and yet this is something that philosophers have commonly ignored or disdained, doubtless because the encounter interests only the person as person; it cannot be universalized, it does not concern the thinking being in general.",
        
        # Para 49
        "It is obvious that such an encounter poses, if you will, a problem each time: but we also see very clearly that the solution of this problem remains on this side of the question that alone matters. Suppose I am told, for example: 'You met such a person in such a place because she loves the same landscapes as you, or because her health obliges her to undergo the treatment you are following yourself'—we see at once that the answer is non-existent. There are in Florence or in the Engadine at the same time as myself a crowd of people who are supposed to share my tastes; there are in the spa where I take the waters a considerable number of patients suffering from the same ailment as myself. But the presumed identity of this taste or this ailment does not bring us together in the real sense of the word; it has no relation to the intimate affinity, unique of its kind, with which we are concerned here. On the other hand, it would be to overstep the limits of valid reasoning to treat this affinity itself as a cause and say: 'It is precisely this affinity that determined our encounter.'",
        
        # Para 50
        "From that moment I find myself in the presence of a mystery, that is to say, of a reality whose roots plunge beyond what is strictly speaking problematic. Shall we elude the difficulty by declaring that after all there is only a lucky chance, a coincidence? A protest immediately rises from the depths of myself against this empty formula, against this ineffective negation of something that I apprehend through the center of myself. But here again we rediscover the initial definition of mystery as a problem encroaching upon its own data: I who question myself on the meaning and possibility of this encounter cannot really place myself outside it or in front of it; I am engaged in this encounter, I depend upon it, I am in some way interior to it, it envelops me and comprehends me—even if I do not comprehend it. It is therefore by a sort of denial or betrayal that I can say: 'After all, that might not have been, I should have remained all the same the one I was, the one I still am.' And one must not say either: 'I was modified by it as by an external cause.' No, it developed me from within, it acted in relation to me as an interior principle to myself."
    ]

